\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces High-level methodological flow adapted from the existing thesis workflow Mermaid diagrams in the repository.}}{33}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Executable system architecture of the repository, rewritten from the repository's Mermaid workflow documentation into a LaTeX-safe diagram. Copyright \textcopyright ~2026\ Daris Dzakwan\ Hoesien.\ \href {https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/}{CC BY 4.0}.}}{34}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Section-level diagram for the data-source stack used by the workflow.}}{35}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Mermaid-derived preprocessing sequence from PDF ingestion to auditable ESG-ready text units.}}{38}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Section-level diagram summarizing how explicit, sparse, contextual, and ontology-aware features are combined.}}{40}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Conceptual split between record generation and validation-comparison within the proposed framework.}}{43}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Layered evaluation-reference construction used when a full expert gold corpus is not yet available.}}{44}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Summary diagram of the chapter's end-to-end methodological contribution.}}{46}{figure.caption.18}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Tone distribution across the active extracted-record layer. This figure supports RQ2 by showing that commitment is the dominant disclosure tone in the current thesis-facing subset.}}{52}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces ESG pillar distribution by tone. This figure shows that environmental and governance disclosures dominate the active evidence layer and helps explain why social-pillar findings remain comparatively weak.}}{52}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Aspect-by-tone heatmap for the active extracted-record layer. This figure supports RQ2 by showing which aspects are concentrated in commitment, action, or outcome language rather than only reporting overall counts.}}{53}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Cross-distribution of LLM tone labels and ClimateBERT-style labels. This figure supports RQ3 by showing where commitment aligns strongly and where action or outcome labels diverge from climate-topic categorization.}}{55}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Failure-mode Pareto chart. This figure shows which small number of failure categories account for most observed extraction weakness in the current thesis-facing subset.}}{57}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Failure-mode composition. This figure shows the relative share of missing tone, schema drift, language ambiguity, layout-related issues, and other recorded failure categories.}}{58}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Model trade-off scatter plot with parse success on the x-axis and average extracted records on the y-axis. This figure helps separate models that are formally stable from those that are practically useful.}}{61}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Prompt-strategy comparison across average records, parse success, and field completion. The figure shows that chain-of-thought and tone-specific framing matter more than generic prompt validity alone.}}{62}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Information-density by tone. This figure shows whether commitment, action, outcome, and none records differ systematically in word count and therefore in extraction complexity.}}{63}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Soft-language ratio by tone. This figure shows whether commitment records contain a higher proportion of soft or future-oriented verbs than action and outcome records, thereby giving a linguistic explanation for some tone-boundary failures.}}{63}{figure.caption.32}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Tone distribution in the active thesis-facing subset. This figure is used to interpret commitment dominance as a substantive disclosure pattern rather than as a descriptive count reported in Chapter 4.}}{68}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces Relationship between LLM tone labels and ClimateBERT-style labels. Reused here because the discussion interprets this cross-distribution as evidence of partial construct overlap rather than full label equivalence.}}{70}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Commitment-outcome gap by company. This figure visualizes the relationship between commitment count, outcome count, and the company-level screening ratio, while the surrounding text keeps clear that the score remains heuristic.}}{72}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces Tone-share ratio chart for the active extracted-record layer. This figure supports the discussion claim that commitment-heavy evidence remains much more common than outcome-heavy evidence in the current subset.}}{73}{figure.caption.37}%
